"Reasoning Tokens - Improve AI Model Decision Making (https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/best-practices/reasoning-tokens)\nciteturn389view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/best-practices/reasoning-tokens\",\"lineno\":321}); Total lines: 699\nL132:   * cite76†Controlling Reasoning Tokens L133:     * cite77†Reasoning tokens and max_tokens L134:     * cite78†Discovering per-model reasoning options L135:     * cite79†Max Tokens for Reasoning L136:     * cite80†Reasoning Effort Level L137:     * cite81†Excluding Reasoning Tokens L138:     * cite82†Enable Reasoning with Default Config L139:     * cite83†Examples L140:     * cite84†Basic Usage with Reasoning Tokens L141:     * cite85†Using Max Tokens for Reasoning L142:     * cite86†Excluding Reasoning Tokens from Response L143:     * cite87†Advanced Usage: Reasoning Chain-of-Thought L144:   * cite88†Preserving Reasoning L145:     * cite89†Example: Preserving Reasoning Blocks with OpenRouter and Claude L146:   * cite90†Reasoning Context Mode L147:   * cite91†Reasoning Mode L148:   * cite92†Reasoning with the Anthropic Messages API L149:   * cite93†Changing Effort Mid-Conversation L150:   * cite94†Reasoning Details API Shape L151:     * cite95†reasoning_details Array Structure L152:     * cite96†Common Fields L153:     * cite97†Reasoning Detail Types L154:     * cite98†Response Examples L155:     * cite99†Non-Streaming Response L156:     * cite100†Streaming Response L157:   * cite101†Legacy Parameters L158:   * cite102†Provider-Specific Reasoning Implementation L159:     * cite103†Anthropic Models with Reasoning Tokens L160:     * cite104†Reasoning Max Tokens for Anthropic Models L161:     * cite105†Summarized Thinking L162:     * cite106†Example: Streaming with Anthropic Reasoning Tokens L163:     * cite107†Google Gemini 3 Models with Thinking Levels L164:     * cite108†Using max_tokens with Gemini 3 L165:     * cite109†Example: Using Thinking Levels with Gemini 3 L166: Best Practices\nL167: # Reasoning Tokens\nL168: \nL169: Copy page Copy page\nL170: For models that support it, the OpenRouter API can return Reasoning Tokens, also known as thinking tokens. OpenRouter normalizes the different ways of customizing the amount of reasoning tokens that the model will use, providing a unified interface across different providers. Reasoning tokens provide a transparent look into the reasoning steps taken by a model. Reasoning tokens are considered output tokens and charged accordingly.\nL171: On most providers they also count against the request’s `max_tokens`; see cite77†Reasoning tokens and max_tokens . Reasoning tokens are included in the response by default if the model decides to output them. Reasoning tokens will appear in the `reasoning` field of each message, unless you decide to exclude them.\nL172: Some reasoning models do not return their reasoning tokens While most models and providers make reasoning tokens available in the response, some (like the OpenAI o-series) do not.\nL173: ##\nL174: \nL175: cite76†​ L176: \nL177: Controlling Reasoning Tokens\nL178: You can control reasoning tokens in your requests using the `reasoning` parameter:\nL179: \nL180:     `{\nL181:       \"model\": \"your-model\",\nL182:       \"messages\": [],\nL183:       \"reasoning\": {\nL184:         // One of the following (not both):\nL185:         \"effort\": \"high\", // Can be \"max\", \"xhigh\", \"high\", \"medium\", \"low\", \"minimal\" or \"none\" (OpenAI-style)\nL186:         \"max_tokens\": 2000, // Specific token limit (Anthropic-style)\nL187: \nL188:         // Optional: Default is false. All models support this.\nL189:         \"exclude\": false, // Set to true to exclude reasoning tokens from response\nL190: \nL191:         // Or enable reasoning with the default parameters:\nL192:         \"enabled\": true // Default: inferred from `effort` or `max_tokens`\nL193:       }\nL194:     }\nL195:     `\nL196: The `reasoning` config object consolidates settings for controlling reasoning strength across different models. See the Note for each option below to see which models are supported and how other models will behave.\nL197: ###\nL198: \nL199: cite77†​ L200: \nL201: Reasoning tokens and max_tokens\nL202: Reasoning tokens count against `max_tokens`On most providers, the request’s `max_tokens` limit (or `max_completion_tokens`, which shares the same budget) applies to reasoning and visible output combined. If the limit is small enough that the model spends all of it reasoning, the response returns `finish_reason: \"length\"` with an empty `content`, and the reasoning tokens are still billed.\nL203: With `reasoning.exclude: true` the `reasoning` field is omitted as well, so the response carries no text at all and the only signal left is `finish_reason` plus `usage`.\nL204: To detect this case, subtract `usage.completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens` from `usage.completion_tokens`. The difference is the number of visible output tokens, and it is 0 or near 0 when reasoning consumed the budget. For example, a request with `max_tokens: 300` that returns `completion_tokens: 302` and `reasoning_tokens: 301` produced a single visible token.\nL205: To avoid it, set `max_tokens` well above the expected reasoning length, or cap reasoning with `reasoning.max_tokens` or a lower `reasoning.effort`. For Anthropic models, `max_tokens` must be strictly higher than the reasoning budget; see cite103†Anthropic Models with Reasoning Tokens .\nL206: ###\nL207: \nL208: cite78†​ L209: \nL210: Discovering per-model reasoning options\nL211: \nL212: Each model in cite110†`GET /api/v1/models` may include a `reasoning` object describing which effort levels it accepts and whether reasoning is mandatory:\nL213: \nL214:     `{\nL215:       \"id\": \"google/gemini-3.5-flash\",\nL216:       \"reasoning\": {\nL217:         \"supported_efforts\": [\"high\", \"medium\", \"low\", \"minimal\"],\nL218:         \"default_effort\": \"medium\",\nL219:         \"default_enabled\": true,\nL220:         \"mandatory\": true\nL221:       }\nL222:     }\nL223:     `\nL224: \nL225: Use this when building client UIs:\nL226:   * `supported_efforts`: Filter effort selectors to these values, returned in descending effort order (highest first). When `null`, all gateway effort values are accepted. When omitted, the model does not expose effort selection.\nL227:   * `default_effort`: Pre-select this effort when enabling reasoning. Maps to `reasoning.effort` in chat requests. If the value is `\"none\"`, treat it as “reasoning off by default” rather than pre-selecting disable when the user explicitly turns reasoning on.\nL228:   * `default_enabled`: Default on/off state when the user has not set `reasoning.enabled`.\nL229:   * `supports_max_tokens`: When present and `true`, show a token budget control and send `reasoning.max_tokens` instead of (or alongside) `reasoning.effort`. Omitted when the model does not support token-budget reasoning.\nL230:   * `mandatory`: When `true`, hide disable controls and do not send `effort: \"none\"` — the model rejects it.\nL231: Non-reasoning models and dynamic router models (`openrouter/auto`, `openrouter/free`) omit the `reasoning` field.\nL232: ###\nL233: \nL234: cite79†​ L235: \nL236: Max Tokens for Reasoning\nL237: \nL238: Supported models Currently supported by:\nL239: \nL240:   * Gemini thinking models\nL241:   * Anthropic reasoning models (by using the `reasoning.max_tokens` parameter)\nL242:   * Some Alibaba Qwen thinking models (mapped to `thinking_budget`)\nL243: For Alibaba, support varies by model — please check the individual model descriptions to confirm whether `reasoning.max_tokens` (via `thinking_budget`) is available.\nL244: \nL245: For models that support reasoning token allocation, you can control it like this:\nL246:   * `\"max_tokens\": 2000` - Directly specifies the maximum number of tokens to use for reasoning\nL247: For models that only support `reasoning.effort` (see below), the `max_tokens` value will be used to determine the effort level.\nL248: ###\nL249: \nL250: cite80†​ L251: \nL252: Reasoning Effort Level\nL253: \nL254: Supported models Currently supported by OpenAI reasoning models (o1 series, o3 series, GPT-5 series) and Grok models\nL255:   * `\"effort\": \"max\"` - Allocates the largest portion of tokens for reasoning (approximately 95% of max_tokens)\nL256:   * `\"effort\": \"xhigh\"` - Same allocation as max (approximately 95% of max_tokens)\nL257:   * `\"effort\": \"high\"` - Allocates a large portion of tokens for reasoning (approximately 80% of max_tokens)\nL258:   * `\"effort\": \"medium\"` - Allocates a moderate portion of tokens (approximately 50% of max_tokens)\nL259:   * `\"effort\": \"low\"` - Allocates a smaller portion of tokens (approximately 20% of max_tokens)\nL260:   * `\"effort\": \"minimal\"` - Allocates an even smaller portion of tokens (approximately 10% of max_tokens)\nL261:   * `\"effort\": \"none\"` - Disables reasoning entirely\nL262: For models that only support `reasoning.max_tokens`, the effort level will be set based on the percentages above.\nL263: ###\nL264: \nL265: cite81†​ L266: \nL267: Excluding Reasoning Tokens\nL268: \nL269: If you want the model to use reasoning internally but not include it in the response:\nL270: \nL271:   * `\"exclude\": true` - The model will still use reasoning, but it won’t be returned in the response. The tokens are still billed and cite77†count against `max_tokens` .\nL272: Reasoning tokens will appear in the `reasoning` field of each message.\nL273: ###\nL274: \nL275: cite82†​ L276: \nL277: Enable Reasoning with Default Config\nL278: \nL279: To enable reasoning with the default parameters:\nL280: \nL281:   * `\"enabled\": true` - Enables reasoning at the “medium” effort level with no exclusions.\nL282: \nL283: ###\nL284: \nL285: cite83†​ L286: \nL287: Examples\nL288: \nL289: ####\nL290: \nL291: cite84†​ L292: \nL293: Basic Usage with Reasoning Tokens\nL294: \nL295: ####\nL296: \nL297: cite85†​ L298: \nL299: Using Max Tokens for Reasoning\nL300: \nL301: For models that support direct token allocation (like Anthropic models), you can specify the exact number of tokens to use for reasoning:\nL302: ####\nL303: \nL304: cite86†​ L305: \nL306: Excluding Reasoning Tokens from Response\nL307: \nL308: If you want the model to use reasoning internally but not include it in the response:\nL309: \nL310: ####\nL311: \nL312: cite87†​ L313: \nL314: Advanced Usage: Reasoning Chain-of-Thought\nL315: \nL316: This example shows how to use reasoning tokens in a more complex workflow. It injects one model’s reasoning into another model to improve its response quality:\nL317: ##\nL318: \nL319: cite88†​ L320: \nL321: Preserving Reasoning\nL322: \nL323: To preserve reasoning context across multiple turns, you can pass it back to the API in one of two ways:\nL324:   1. `message.reasoning` (string): Pass the plaintext reasoning as a string field on the assistant message\nL325:   2. `message.reasoning_details` (array): Pass the full reasoning_details block\nL326: Use `reasoning_details` when working with models that return special reasoning types (such as encrypted or summarized) - this preserves the full structure needed for those models. For models that only return raw reasoning strings, you can use the simpler `reasoning` field. You can also use `reasoning_content` as an alias - it functions identically to `reasoning`. The `reasoning_details` functionality works identically across all supported reasoning models.\nL327: You can easily switch between OpenAI reasoning models (like `~openai/gpt-sol-latest`) and Anthropic reasoning models (like `~anthropic/claude-sonnet-latest`) without changing your code structure. Preserving reasoning blocks is useful specifically for tool calling. When models like Claude invoke tools, it is pausing its construction of a response to await external information. When tool results are returned, the model will continue building that existing response.\nL328: This necessitates preserving reasoning blocks during tool use, for a couple of reasons: Reasoning continuity: The reasoning blocks capture the model’s step-by-step reasoning that led to tool requests. When you post tool results, including the original reasoning ensures the model can continue its reasoning from where it left off. Context maintenance: While tool results appear as user messages in the API structure, they’re part of a continuous reasoning flow.\nL329: Preserving reasoning blocks maintains this conceptual flow across multiple API calls.\nL330: Important for Reasoning Models When providing reasoning_details blocks, the entire sequence of consecutive reasoning blocks must match the outputs generated by the model during the original request; you cannot rearrange or modify the sequence of these blocks.\nL331: ###\nL332: \nL333: cite89†​ L334: \nL335: Example: Preserving Reasoning Blocks with OpenRouter and Claude\nL336: \nL337: For more detailed information about thinking encryption, redacted blocks, and advanced use cases, see cite111†Anthropic’s documentation on extended thinking†docs.anthropic.com . For more information about OpenAI reasoning models, see cite112†OpenAI’s reasoning documentation†platform.openai.com .\nL338: ##\nL339: \nL340: cite90†​ L341: \nL342: Reasoning Context Mode\nL343: \nL344: When you echo reasoning items back in your conversation history, you can control which reasoning the model has access to with the `reasoning.context` parameter:\nL345:   * `auto`: The model uses its default context mode. Omitting `reasoning.context` has the same effect.\nL346:   * `all_turns`: The model can reference reasoning from all turns present in the input. Use this for multi-turn conversations where you want the model to build on its previous chain of thought.\nL347:   * `current_turn`: The model only uses reasoning from the current turn. Prior reasoning items in the input are ignored. Use this when you want a fresh reasoning pass without influence from earlier turns.\nL348: `reasoning.context` is only supported by OpenAI GPT-5.6 and newer.\nL349: When using the Responses API with manual state management (echoing output items back as input), set `reasoning.context` alongside `include`:\nL350: \nL351:     `{\nL352:       \"model\": \"~openai/gpt-sol-latest\",\nL353:       \"input\": [\nL354:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Solve this math problem step by step: ...\" },\nL355:         {\nL356:           \"type\": \"reasoning\",\nL357:           \"id\": \"rs_abc123\",\nL358:           \"encrypted_content\": \"...\",\nL359:           \"summary\": [{ \"type\": \"summary_text\", \"text\": \"Analyzed the equation...\" }]\nL360:         },\nL361:         {\nL362:           \"type\": \"message\",\nL363:           \"role\": \"assistant\",\nL364:           \"content\": [{ \"type\": \"output_text\", \"text\": \"The answer is 42.\" }]\nL365:         },\nL366:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Now explain it differently.\" }\nL367:       ],\nL368:       \"reasoning\": {\nL369:         \"effort\": \"high\",\nL370:         \"context\": \"all_turns\"\nL371:       },\nL372:       \"include\": [\"reasoning.encrypted_content\"]\nL373:     }\nL374:     `\nL375: The default value of `context` may vary by model. Set it explicitly if your use case requires a specific behavior.\nL376: ##\nL377: \nL378: cite91†​ L379: \nL380: Reasoning Mode\nL381: \nL382: For models that offer a pro reasoning variant, the `reasoning.mode` parameter controls which variant serves your request:\nL383: \nL384:   * `standard`: The model’s standard reasoning behavior. Omitting `reasoning.mode` has the same effect.\nL385:   * `pro`: Routes the request to the model’s pro variant, which uses deeper multi-pass reasoning for harder problems.\nL386: `reasoning.mode` is only supported by OpenAI GPT-5.6 and newer, when served by OpenAI or Azure. Amazon Bedrock’s OpenAI-compatible API accepts the field but silently ignores it, so pro reasoning is not available on Bedrock — OpenRouter only routes pro requests to providers that honor mode selection.\nL387: \nL388: For each supported model, there are two equivalent ways to request pro mode on OpenRouter:\nL389:   1. Send `reasoning.mode: \"pro\"` with the standard model — OpenRouter reroutes the request to the matching `*-pro` model.\nL390:   2. Call the `*-pro` model listing directly.\nL391: \nL392:     `{\nL393:       \"model\": \"~openai/gpt-sol-latest\",\nL394:       \"input\": \"Prove that there are infinitely many primes.\",\nL395:       \"reasoning\": {\nL396:         \"mode\": \"pro\"\nL397:       }\nL398:     }\nL399:     `\nL400:   * `mode` is independent of `effort`: you can combine `mode: \"pro\"` with any supported request-level effort. It cannot be combined with a cite113†mid-conversation effort update , which OpenAI does not accept on pro models, so that combination is rejected with a 400.\nL401:   * Pro mode bills at the same per-token rates as standard mode, but typically consumes more tokens.\nL402: ##\nL403: \nL404: cite92†​ L405: \nL406: Reasoning with the Anthropic Messages API\nL407: \nL408: Requests sent through the cite114†Anthropic Messages API control reasoning with Anthropic’s native `thinking` and `output_config.effort` fields. OpenRouter normalizes both into the unified `reasoning` parameter, so they work on every reasoning model, not only Claude.\nL409: Messages API request  | Unified `reasoning` equivalent\nL410: --- | ---\nL411: `output_config: { effort }`  | `reasoning: { effort }`\nL412: `thinking: { type: \"enabled\", budget_tokens }`  | `reasoning: { max_tokens: budget_tokens }`\nL413: `thinking: { type: \"adaptive\" }`  | `reasoning: { enabled: true }`\nL414: `thinking: { type: \"disabled\" }`  | `reasoning: { enabled: false }`\nL415: `output_config.effort` takes precedence over `thinking.type: \"adaptive\"`. Sending `thinking: { type: \"adaptive\" }` together with `output_config: { effort: \"high\" }` means high effort on every model. Claude receives both fields unchanged, while a model such as GPT-5 or MiniMax receives `reasoning_effort: \"high\"` in its own vocabulary, translated the same way as request-level `reasoning.effort`.\nL416: When `thinking: { type: \"enabled\", budget_tokens }` and `output_config.effort` are sent together, Claude receives both unchanged. The unified `reasoning` parameter accepts either `effort` or `max_tokens` but not both, so every other model receives `reasoning.effort` and the budget is not forwarded. Send only `thinking.budget_tokens` to control other models by token budget. `thinking: { type: \"adaptive\" }` without an `output_config.effort` leaves the effort to the model.\nL417: Claude runs its native adaptive thinking, and other models use their default reasoning effort, which the models endpoint reports as cite78†`reasoning.default_effort` . `thinking: { type: \"disabled\" }` disables reasoning even when an `output_config.effort` is also present. Claude still receives the `output_config.effort`, since Anthropic applies it to the whole response, and other models receive no reasoning effort.\nL418: OpenAI models expose two knobs, `reasoning.effort` and `text.verbosity`, and the Messages API request shape has one. `output_config.effort` maps to `reasoning.effort` only, and it is not sent as `text.verbosity`. Anthropic’s `xhigh` and `max` levels stay reasoning efforts and are translated to the model’s cite78†supported reasoning efforts . To set OpenAI response verbosity, use the cite115†`verbosity` parameter on the Chat Completions or Responses API.\nL419: ##\nL420: \nL421: cite93†​ L422: \nL423: Changing Effort Mid-Conversation\nL424: Request-level effort applies to the whole conversation, so raising it for one hard turn and lowering it again on the next changes the request prefix and invalidates the prompt cache. Some models instead accept an effort change as an item in the conversation history. The change applies from that point onward and stays in effect until the next change, the preceding items are untouched, and the cache stays warm. Keep the item at the same position in every later request.\nL425: All three OpenRouter APIs expose this, each in the shape native to that API. The Chat Completions form is an OpenRouter extension, since OpenAI’s own Chat Completions API has no per-message equivalent.\nL426: \nL427:     `// Chat Completions\nL428:     {\nL429:       \"model\": \"anthropic/claude-fable-5.1\",\nL430:       \"reasoning\": { \"effort\": \"high\" },\nL431:       \"messages\": [\nL432:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Plan the migration.\" },\nL433:         { \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Here's the plan: ...\" },\nL434:         { \"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"\", \"configuration_update\": { \"reasoning\": { \"effort\": \"low\" } } },\nL435:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Now rename the config file.\" }\nL436:       ]\nL437:     }\nL438:     `\nL439: \nL440:     `// Responses API\nL441:     {\nL442:       \"model\": \"anthropic/claude-fable-5.1\",\nL443:       \"reasoning\": { \"effort\": \"high\" },\nL444:       \"input\": [\nL445:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Plan the migration.\" },\nL446:         { \"type\": \"message\", \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [{ \"type\": \"output_text\", \"text\": \"Here's the plan: ...\" }] },\nL447:         { \"type\": \"configuration_update\", \"reasoning\": { \"effort\": \"low\" } },\nL448:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Now rename the config file.\" }\nL449:       ]\nL450:     }\nL451:     `\nL452: \nL453:     `// Anthropic Messages API\nL454:     {\nL455:       \"model\": \"anthropic/claude-fable-5.1\",\nL456:       \"max_tokens\": 4096,\nL457:       \"output_config\": { \"effort\": \"high\" },\nL458:       \"messages\": [\nL459:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Plan the migration.\" },\nL460:         { \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Here's the plan: ...\" },\nL461:         { \"role\": \"system\", \"content\": [], \"output_config\": { \"effort\": \"low\" } },\nL462:         { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Now rename the config file.\" }\nL463:       ]\nL464:     }\nL465:     `\nL466: The three forms are interchangeable: OpenRouter normalizes each into one internal representation and re-emits it in the form the selected provider accepts. A Responses `configuration_update` sent to a Claude model becomes a per-message `output_config`, and a Chat Completions or Messages API update sent to an OpenAI model becomes a Responses `configuration_update` item.\nL467: The request-level effort (`reasoning.effort` or `output_config.effort`) is independent of the per-item updates and still sets the baseline for turns before the first update. Effort values are translated to the target model’s vocabulary the same way as request-level effort. Claude accepts `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max` (`minimal` is sent as `low`, `none` is rejected).\nL468: OpenAI models accept the efforts listed in their cite78†supported reasoning efforts , and an update with an effort the model does not support is rejected with a 400.\nL469:   * Put the update directly before the user turn it should apply to, on a content-less system message (Chat Completions and Messages API) or a `configuration_update` item (Responses API). An update ahead of the first user turn is allowed; on Claude models it stays inline in `messages` while ordinary system text is still sent as the top-level `system` prompt, so a system message that carries both text and an update is split in two.\nL470:   * Do not place two updates next to each other. The first would apply to nothing; OpenAI rejects adjacent `configuration_update` items, and OpenRouter applies the same rule on every API so the three forms stay interchangeable.\nL471:   * An update after the last user turn is accepted and applies to the response generated by that request. Keep it in the same position in later requests so the cached prefix still matches.\nL472:   * Updates cannot be combined with `truncation: \"auto\"` in the Responses API, and on any API they cannot be combined with `reasoning.mode: \"pro\"` or sent to a pro model variant such as `openai/gpt-6-astra-pro`.\nL473:   * Mid-conversation effort updates are not accepted on the cite23†Batch API and are rejected per line.\nL474: Support is per model and OpenRouter enables it as providers roll it out; the first models to accept mid-conversation effort changes were Claude Fable 5.1 and OpenAI GPT-6 Astra, and Claude Opus 5 accepts them as well. Requests that use an update are routed only to endpoints that accept it, and a request for a model that does not support it is rejected with a 400 instead of being sent with the update silently dropped.\nL475: ##\nL476: \nL477: cite94†​ L478: \nL479: Reasoning Details API Shape\nL480: \nL481: When reasoning models generate responses, the reasoning information is structured in a standardized format through the `reasoning_details` array. This section documents the API response structure for reasoning details in both streaming and non-streaming responses.\nL482: ###\nL483: \nL484: cite95†​ L485: \nL486: reasoning_details Array Structure\nL487: \nL488: The `reasoning_details` field contains an array of reasoning detail objects. Each object in the array represents a specific piece of reasoning information and follows one of three possible types. The location of this array differs between streaming and non-streaming responses.\nL489:   * Non-streaming responses: `reasoning_details` appears in `choices[].message.reasoning_details`\nL490:   * Streaming responses: `reasoning_details` appears in `choices[].delta.reasoning_details` for each chunk\nL491: ####\nL492: \nL493: cite96†​ L494: \nL495: Common Fields\nL496: \nL497: All reasoning detail objects share these common fields:\nL498:   * `id` (string | null): Unique identifier for the reasoning detail\nL499:   * `format` (string): The format of the reasoning detail, with possible values:\nL500:     * `\"unknown\"` - Format is not specified\nL501:     * `\"openai-responses-v1\"` - OpenAI responses format version 1\nL502:     * `\"azure-openai-responses-v1\"` - Azure OpenAI responses format version 1\nL503:     * `\"bedrock-openai-responses-v1\"` - Amazon Bedrock OpenAI responses format version 1\nL504:     * `\"bedrock-xai-responses-v1\"` - Amazon Bedrock xAI responses format version 1\nL505:     * `\"xai-responses-v1\"` - SpaceXAI responses format version 1\nL506:     * `\"meta-responses-v1\"` - Meta responses format version 1\nL507:     * `\"anthropic-claude-v1\"` - Anthropic Claude format version 1 (default)\nL508:     * `\"google-gemini-v1\"` - Google Gemini format version 1\nL509:   * `index` (number, optional): Sequential index of the reasoning detail\nL510: ####\nL511: \nL512: cite97†​ L513: \nL514: Reasoning Detail Types\nL515: \nL516: 1. Summary Type (`reasoning.summary`) Contains a high-level summary of the reasoning process:\nL517: \nL518:     `{\nL519:       \"type\": \"reasoning.summary\",\nL520:       \"summary\": \"The model analyzed the problem by first identifying key constraints, then evaluating possible solutions...\",\nL521:       \"id\": \"reasoning-summary-1\",\nL522:       \"format\": \"anthropic-claude-v1\",\nL523:       \"index\": 0\nL524:     }\nL525:     `\nL526: 2. Encrypted Type (`reasoning.encrypted`) Contains encrypted reasoning data that may be redacted or protected:\nL527: \nL528:     `{\nL529:       \"type\": \"reasoning.encrypted\",\nL530:       \"data\": \"eyJlbmNyeXB0ZWQiOiJ0cnVlIiwiY29udGVudCI6IltSRURBQ1RFRF0ifQ==\",\nL531:       \"id\": \"reasoning-encrypted-1\",\nL532:       \"format\": \"anthropic-claude-v1\",\nL533:       \"index\": 1\nL534:     }\nL535:     `\nL536: 3. Text Type (`reasoning.text`) Contains raw text reasoning with optional signature verification:\nL537: \nL538:     `{\nL539:       \"type\": \"reasoning.text\",\nL540:       \"text\": \"Let me think through this step by step:\\n 1. First, I need to understand the user's question...\",\nL541:       \"signature\": \"sha256:abc123def456...\",\nL542:       \"id\": \"reasoning-text-1\",\nL543:       \"format\": \"anthropic-claude-v1\",\nL544:       \"index\": 2\nL545:     }\nL546:     `\nL547: ###\nL548: \nL549: cite98†​ L550: \nL551: Response Examples\nL552: ####\nL553: \nL554: cite99†​ L555: \nL556: Non-Streaming Response\nL557: In non-streaming responses, `reasoning_details` appears in the message:\nL558: \nL559:     `{\nL560:       \"choices\": [\nL561:         {\nL562:           \"message\": {\nL563:             \"role\": \"assistant\",\nL564:             \"content\": \"Based on my analysis, I recommend the following approach...\",\nL565:             \"reasoning_details\": [\nL566:               {\nL567:                 \"type\": \"reasoning.summary\",\nL568:                 \"summary\": \"Analyzed the problem by breaking it into components\",\nL569:                 \"id\": \"reasoning-summary-1\",\nL570:                 \"format\": \"anthropic-claude-v1\",\nL571:                 \"index\": 0\nL572:               },\nL573:               {\nL574:                 \"type\": \"reasoning.text\",\nL575:                 \"text\": \"Let me work through this systematically:\\n 1. First consideration...\\n 2. Second consideration...\",\nL576:                 \"signature\": null,\nL577:                 \"id\": \"reasoning-text-1\",\nL578:                 \"format\": \"anthropic-claude-v1\",\nL579:                 \"index\": 1\nL580:               }\nL581:             ]\nL582:           }\nL583:         }\nL584:       ]\nL585:     }\nL586:     `\nL587: See all 27 lines\nL588: ####\nL589: \nL590: cite100†​ L591: \nL592: Streaming Response\nL593: In streaming responses, `reasoning_details` appears in delta chunks as the reasoning is generated:\nL594: \nL595:     `{\nL596:       \"choices\": [\nL597:         {\nL598:           \"delta\": {\nL599:             \"reasoning_details\": [\nL600:               {\nL601:                 \"type\": \"reasoning.text\",\nL602:                 \"text\": \"Let me think about this step by step...\",\nL603:                 \"signature\": null,\nL604:                 \"id\": \"reasoning-text-1\",\nL605:                 \"format\": \"anthropic-claude-v1\",\nL606:                 \"index\": 0\nL607:               }\nL608:             ]\nL609:           }\nL610:         }\nL611:       ]\nL612:     }\nL613:     `\nL614: Streaming Behavior Notes:\nL615: \nL616:   * Each reasoning detail chunk is sent as it becomes available\nL617:   * The `reasoning_details` array in each chunk may contain one or more reasoning objects\nL618:   * For encrypted reasoning, the content may appear as `[REDACTED]` in streaming responses\nL619:   * The complete reasoning sequence is built by concatenating all chunks in order\nL620: ##\nL621: \nL622: cite101†​ L623: \nL624: Legacy Parameters\nL625: \nL626: For backward compatibility, OpenRouter still supports the following legacy parameters:\nL627: \nL628:   * `include_reasoning: true` - Equivalent to `reasoning: {}`\nL629:   * `include_reasoning: false` - Equivalent to `reasoning: { exclude: true }`\nL630: However, we recommend using the new unified `reasoning` parameter for better control and future compatibility.\nL631: \nL632: ##\nL633: \nL634: cite102†​ L635: \nL636: Provider-Specific Reasoning Implementation\nL637: ###\nL638: \nL639: cite103†​ L640: \nL641: Anthropic Models with Reasoning Tokens\nL642: \n"
